{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CghAcc4fMHQeA57VpZzr6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "For identity resolution, AuditService relies on **UserService**, and UserService in turn relies on **UsersPostgresDatabase**.\n\n**Dependency chain:**\n\n1. **AuditService \u2192 UserService** (DEPENDS_ON). This is the only service AuditService depends on directly, so it is where identity resolution happens. The graph doesn't use the words \"identity resolution.\" I'm treating UserService as the identity provider because it's the user service and AuditService's only dependency.\n2. **UserService \u2192 UsersPostgresDatabase** (STORES). The user records UserService looks up are stored here, so AuditService depends on this database indirectly.\n\n**Configuration:**\n- **ORDER_DB_URL** configures UserService's read replica. The name is misleading: it says \"ORDER\" but it points at the user data. The graph says the old name was kept because three deployment scripts read it. The sentence that records this starts with \"It is configured by\u2026\", so the graph's assignment of this key to UserService comes from how that \"It\" was read. I'd check that against the actual deployment config before changing the key.\n- No configuration keys are recorded for AuditService itself.\n\n**Not part of identity resolution:**\n- **AuditIcebergTable** is where AuditService writes its audit records (STORES). That's storage, not identity.\n\nIf UserService, UsersPostgresDatabase, or ORDER_DB_URL breaks, AuditService can't resolve user identities."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 355, "cache_read_input_tokens": 4839, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 355}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "i